1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
He designed planes, tanks, and even robots.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:07,000
All in the 15th century.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
By the time he was done, he'd constructed cities,

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:13,000
rerouted rivers, and mapped the human body.

5
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:16,000
He even took the time to do a little painting.

6
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:19,000
Not bad for a self-taught country boy.

7
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:21,000
How did he do it?

8
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:24,000
And do his inventions really work?

9
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:28,000
Now, Da Vinci Tech on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
War in the 21st century is devastating and explosive.

11
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:52,180
No target is secure even hundreds of miles away.

12
00:00:54,660 --> 00:00:57,960
Today's high-tech weapons are the culmination of a transformation

13
00:00:57,961 --> 00:01:00,360
that began nearly 500 years ago,

14
00:01:00,460 --> 00:01:04,800
during the age of Leonardo da Vinci,

15
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:06,840
at the height of the Italian Renaissance.

16
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:13,240
Today, Leonardo was most famous for the beauty and mystery

17
00:01:13,241 --> 00:01:13,941
of his paintings.

18
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:17,920
But for most of his life, Leonardo was known equally well

19
00:01:17,921 --> 00:01:19,220
as a military engineer.

20
00:01:22,820 --> 00:01:26,780
Leonardo was fascinated with war as a physical activity.

21
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:33,500
Not the killing of people, but the dynamics of war.

22
00:01:34,060 --> 00:01:39,960
The attackers, the defenders, all the physical properties of throwing things at each other,

23
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:46,080
whether they be ancient ideas like the trebuchet or the ballista or the catapult,

24
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:49,220
or new ideas like cannons.

25
00:01:52,660 --> 00:01:57,500
How the Renaissance's most famous artist became renowned as a creator of war machines

26
00:01:57,501 --> 00:02:03,160
reveals much about Leonardo the man, the inventor, and the world in which he lived.

27
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:11,260
Leonardo was born out of wedlock in 1452

28
00:02:11,261 --> 00:02:17,300
in the small rural town of Vinci, 22 miles outside of Florence, Italy.

29
00:02:19,060 --> 00:02:22,080
Here he developed a lifelong fascination with nature,

30
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:25,600
which he would draw with legendary skill.

31
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:32,340
But his illegitimate birth denied Leonardo the right to a formal education,

32
00:02:32,900 --> 00:02:36,180
and later excluded him from the most lucrative occupations in Florence.

33
00:02:39,160 --> 00:02:43,260
Throughout his career, Leonardo's success would depend on his wit and talent,

34
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:47,940
and the patronage of the era's richest, most powerful, and often dangerous men.

35
00:02:48,820 --> 00:02:55,360
I think he was a man of ambition, and he wanted to get ahead to move out of his rural background

36
00:02:55,361 --> 00:02:58,500
and of his illegitimate background,

37
00:02:58,501 --> 00:03:00,580
and to make a name for himself.

38
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:04,120
And it certainly started in Florence.

39
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:08,160
He would try all sorts of means of learning,

40
00:03:08,360 --> 00:03:09,500
of promoting himself.

41
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:14,580
Artist, architect, inventor.

42
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:19,160
Leonardo pursued many vocations throughout his life.

43
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:23,720
In 1482, at the age of 30,

44
00:03:23,721 --> 00:03:26,280
He took the biggest risk of his career,

45
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:30,580
abandoning his hometown of Florence for a new city and new opportunities.

46
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:34,660
One of the things that Leonardo did that shocked his contemporaries

47
00:03:34,661 --> 00:03:37,820
and still shocks some modern people even today

48
00:03:37,821 --> 00:03:42,660
is that he moved from Florence, the cultural capital of northern Italy,

49
00:03:42,661 --> 00:03:47,940
to Milan, which was clearly the political and military bad boy

50
00:03:47,941 --> 00:03:50,540
of all of the Italian city-states.

51
00:03:50,541 --> 00:03:55,180
He wanted a broader scope for his work.

52
00:03:56,980 --> 00:04:00,020
Milan was a place that lived and died by the sword,

53
00:04:00,820 --> 00:04:03,380
a reflection of its leader, Ludovico Sforza,

54
00:04:03,780 --> 00:04:05,940
known as Il Moro, the Dark One.

55
00:04:06,580 --> 00:04:11,580
He sold his services to Duke Ludovico as a military engineer,

56
00:04:12,060 --> 00:04:15,520
despite the fact that he had no training in that particular area.

57
00:04:15,860 --> 00:04:19,040
But he was brilliant, he knew what the client would like,

58
00:04:19,041 --> 00:04:21,000
and he certainly had a number of ideas

59
00:04:21,001 --> 00:04:24,880
that any militarily ambitious ruler would like.

60
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:29,500
Whatever the era,

61
00:04:30,020 --> 00:04:32,380
mobility is crucial to victory on the battlefield.

62
00:04:35,020 --> 00:04:36,740
To impress his new patron,

63
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:39,220
Leonardo proposed mobile bridges,

64
00:04:41,620 --> 00:04:42,480
covered ladders,

65
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:47,400
even assault boats, guaranteed, as he wrote,

66
00:04:47,401 --> 00:04:49,400
to cause great confusion in the enemy.

67
00:04:52,900 --> 00:04:56,120
He understood better than most of his contemporaries

68
00:04:56,121 --> 00:04:58,260
the psychological effect of weapons

69
00:04:58,261 --> 00:05:01,480
and realized that war is, at bottom,

70
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:04,020
a kind of psychological exercise.

71
00:05:05,660 --> 00:05:09,480
It's much more efficient to terrify your enemy

72
00:05:09,481 --> 00:05:11,020
and to cause him to flee

73
00:05:11,021 --> 00:05:14,440
than to have to kill and kill and kill again

74
00:05:14,441 --> 00:05:17,600
in order to be victorious on the battlefield.

75
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:20,100
And I think many of his weapons were designed,

76
00:05:20,420 --> 00:05:22,500
at least in part, with this in mind.

77
00:05:24,100 --> 00:05:25,900
Of all Leonardo's designs,

78
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:28,980
none was more intimidating than his giant crossbow,

79
00:05:29,420 --> 00:05:32,300
a battlefield colossus sure to get the enemy's attention.

80
00:05:33,140 --> 00:05:35,460
And the details he provided are fascinating.

81
00:05:36,960 --> 00:05:39,760
The crossbow was to measure 42 braccia,

82
00:05:40,180 --> 00:05:42,240
or roughly 27 yards across.

83
00:05:42,241 --> 00:05:48,480
The bows were made of layers of thin wood to increase their flexibility and create greater firepower.

84
00:05:48,481 --> 00:05:53,120
Many have mistaken the dark lines on the central shaft for a large arrow.

85
00:05:53,121 --> 00:06:00,960
But the pouch at the end of the double bow strings suggest the true projectile to be of heavy stone, or perhaps a flaming bomb.

86
00:06:05,520 --> 00:06:10,240
Those lines are in fact a worm screw mechanism used to arm the crossbow.

87
00:06:10,241 --> 00:06:14,480
A block along the mechanism is moved forward to engage the bow strings.

88
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:18,400
A soldier then cranks the bow backwards using the worm screw.

89
00:06:19,160 --> 00:06:23,120
Firing the weapon required the soldier to either slam the block down with a heavy mallet

90
00:06:23,121 --> 00:06:26,140
or lever the bow strings upward with a crowbar.

91
00:06:27,780 --> 00:06:30,360
It's so persuasive that you think it would work,

92
00:06:30,680 --> 00:06:33,900
but if you look at it a little bit, you wonder whether it would work.

93
00:06:33,901 --> 00:06:42,460
But he makes his art, makes the impossible or the improbable into something that is possible.

94
00:06:42,740 --> 00:06:47,300
It's why his notebooks and so many of his experiments on paper are so interesting.

95
00:06:47,600 --> 00:06:49,480
Because what are they? Are they art and imagination?

96
00:06:49,960 --> 00:06:52,540
Or are they observation of how things should work?

97
00:06:52,780 --> 00:06:56,320
Are these things real? Or are they projections of what might be?

98
00:06:58,060 --> 00:07:02,260
In 15th century Europe, the use of gunpowder was in its infancy.

99
00:07:03,160 --> 00:07:08,020
Yet Leonardo's drawings anticipate a battlefield dominated by heavy and light artillery,

100
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:10,500
armored vehicles,

101
00:07:11,100 --> 00:07:14,920
and multi-firing weapons.

102
00:07:17,160 --> 00:07:20,860
Technology that didn't emerge until the 19th and 20th centuries.

103
00:07:22,240 --> 00:07:27,160
And the solutions he foresaw were often startling examples of his mechanical ingenuity.

104
00:07:27,161 --> 00:07:33,320
The problem with firearms on the battlefield is that their reload times are much too long,

105
00:07:33,400 --> 00:07:34,920
and there's no way around this.

106
00:07:35,260 --> 00:07:40,140
You can't really speed up the reload cycle for these kinds of guns appreciably.

107
00:07:40,540 --> 00:07:44,320
So the only way they're really useful is in mass amounts.

108
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:48,740
To Leonardo, the answer was to build multi-barreled guns,

109
00:07:49,240 --> 00:07:53,260
which were mobile and could allow the gunner to create a volley of fire before reloading.

110
00:07:53,261 --> 00:08:03,040
In his most spectacular design, Leonardo proposed 33 small-caliber gun barrels, organized in three rows of 11 each.

111
00:08:04,780 --> 00:08:07,780
All three rows were mounted on a single revolving framework.

112
00:08:09,440 --> 00:08:14,900
Once the first row of ammunition had been fired, the gunner could easily set up the second and third rows to follow.

113
00:08:16,200 --> 00:08:20,140
Meanwhile, the expired rows could be either reloaded or allowed time to cool.

114
00:08:20,141 --> 00:08:24,160
clearly a precursor of the modern machine gun.

115
00:08:25,320 --> 00:08:27,800
But of all the weapons that Leonardo conceived,

116
00:08:28,460 --> 00:08:32,120
none would have altered battlefield combat more than his covered chariot,

117
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:35,300
what we now call Leonardo's tank.

118
00:08:36,820 --> 00:08:41,160
In assaulting a fortified position that's heavily defended,

119
00:08:41,700 --> 00:08:44,420
the attacker is going to take a lot of casualties.

120
00:08:45,020 --> 00:08:47,120
This is very encouraging to the defender.

121
00:08:47,121 --> 00:08:57,320
A covered wagon, a sort of tank-like vehicle propelled from the inside and impervious to most of the weapons that most defenders would have,

122
00:08:57,500 --> 00:09:03,920
is going to get through and get up to the base of the wall, and that's going to be terrifying to the defenders.

123
00:09:04,780 --> 00:09:11,120
Like many of Leonardo's conceptions, the tank was inspired by an object in nature, in this case, a tortoiseshell.

124
00:09:11,121 --> 00:09:18,640
Its exterior was reinforced with metal plates and slanted to deflect enemy fire.

125
00:09:19,580 --> 00:09:23,160
Small portholes in the tank's walls allowed men inside to return fire.

126
00:09:24,540 --> 00:09:29,640
A similar set of portholes in the upper turret gave the tank commander a full scope of the battlefield

127
00:09:29,641 --> 00:09:32,500
and allowed him to direct the tank's forward progress.

128
00:09:33,520 --> 00:09:38,800
Power for the tank was born on the backs of eight men who used a series of cranks to turn the wheels.

129
00:09:39,760 --> 00:09:43,780
Interestingly, Leonardo's design featured the cranks running counter to one another,

130
00:09:44,180 --> 00:09:45,860
making forward progress impossible.

131
00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:50,740
It's a novice's mistake, which someone of Leonardo's talent wouldn't have made.

132
00:09:51,780 --> 00:09:55,120
Some have suggested that Leonardo sabotaged his own design

133
00:09:55,121 --> 00:09:59,400
because he was a pacifist at heart and realized the tank's deadly potential.

134
00:10:00,540 --> 00:10:02,200
But there's another explanation.

135
00:10:02,201 --> 00:10:09,100
As an artisan, he didn't want any of his ideas stolen by others.

136
00:10:09,320 --> 00:10:12,660
You know, there were no copyright laws in those days,

137
00:10:12,960 --> 00:10:16,180
and he didn't want these ideas published.

138
00:10:16,300 --> 00:10:20,900
He wanted them for himself and perhaps eventually for his patrons.

139
00:10:21,760 --> 00:10:26,920
This possibly intentional design flaw was only one technique that Leonardo may have used

140
00:10:26,921 --> 00:10:30,160
to ensure that his creations remained under his control.

141
00:10:32,200 --> 00:10:34,600
Although there are many theories about its origin,

142
00:10:35,320 --> 00:10:38,600
Leonardo's famous mirror writing was clearly another deterrent,

143
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:42,960
challenging the overly inquisitive to try to read his words and sentences backwards.

144
00:10:43,420 --> 00:10:48,180
The fact that they were mirror written in that almost illegible left-handed scrawl of his

145
00:10:48,181 --> 00:10:52,340
indicates that they were really designed for his use only.

146
00:10:53,800 --> 00:10:55,840
Such strategies must have worked,

147
00:10:55,841 --> 00:10:59,680
because Leonardo's skills as a military engineer were always in demand.

148
00:10:59,681 --> 00:11:05,640
For 17 years, Il Moro, the Duke of Milan, was his most reliable patron.

149
00:11:06,180 --> 00:11:10,720
But later in life, Leonardo worked for clients including generals in Venice,

150
00:11:11,380 --> 00:11:16,700
the Pope in Rome, and the bloodthirsty Florentine, Cesare Borgia.

151
00:11:17,580 --> 00:11:19,580
Cesare Borgia was in many ways a psychopath.

152
00:11:20,240 --> 00:11:23,640
He was ruthless, violent, vicious, faithless,

153
00:11:23,641 --> 00:11:29,300
and had very little to actually commend him as anything except a successful military commander.

154
00:11:29,680 --> 00:11:36,500
For Borgia, Leonardo created detailed maps of the Italian countryside, crucial tools

155
00:11:36,501 --> 00:11:41,120
for the strongman's ambition to control all of central Italy.

156
00:11:41,121 --> 00:11:45,700
And Leonardo offered sophisticated ideas for new fortifications to counter the growing

157
00:11:45,701 --> 00:11:51,260
threat from mortar and cannon.

158
00:11:51,261 --> 00:11:56,340
Walls would be angled to deflect incoming artillery shells, while built-in cannon platforms were

159
00:11:56,341 --> 00:12:00,340
added at key junctures to allow return fire.

160
00:12:00,341 --> 00:12:08,940
For many of Leonardo's patrons, war became an art unto itself.

161
00:12:08,941 --> 00:12:16,320
And Leonardo had used his war machines as a calling card to gain their favor.

162
00:12:16,321 --> 00:12:23,620
But when he had a choice, Leonardo pursued many other interests, including what are now

163
00:12:23,621 --> 00:12:28,620
now known as Leonardo's Lost Robots.

164
00:12:31,800 --> 00:12:35,240
In addition to devising his own recipe for gunpowder,

165
00:12:35,241 --> 00:12:38,700
Leonardo designed cannon shells that exploded on impact,

166
00:12:38,701 --> 00:12:42,240
or divided into smaller explosives while still in flight.

167
00:12:42,241 --> 00:12:45,240
Da Vinci Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

168
00:12:45,241 --> 00:12:56,960
In January 2004, twin robots landed on Mars.

169
00:12:58,860 --> 00:13:03,920
The latest in NASA's long-term effort to explore the Red Planet using robotic devices.

170
00:13:05,700 --> 00:13:11,520
Self-propelled and controlled by computers, the Mars rovers represent the pinnacle of automation

171
00:13:11,521 --> 00:13:16,880
and the power of machines to extend the reach of man to wherever he might dream.

172
00:13:18,340 --> 00:13:21,580
But few people realize that 500 years ago,

173
00:13:22,160 --> 00:13:25,260
Leonardo da Vinci was already a pioneer of robotic devices.

174
00:13:26,200 --> 00:13:28,560
Used for entertaining and honoring his patrons,

175
00:13:29,360 --> 00:13:32,660
Leonardo's robots reveal the facility of his mechanical mind.

176
00:13:33,220 --> 00:13:37,920
They are the ultimate expression of talents that emerged while he was still a young man in Florence.

177
00:13:37,921 --> 00:13:44,620
He entered the scene in a period and in a place which was blessed by God in a sense.

178
00:13:44,900 --> 00:13:50,160
Florence, end of the 15th century, fantastic site to develop skills and talents.

179
00:13:52,360 --> 00:13:55,900
Florence in the 15th century was the heart and soul of the Renaissance,

180
00:13:56,680 --> 00:14:00,560
a place where the creative energies of art, science and politics

181
00:14:00,561 --> 00:14:05,120
were fused in great public works that dominate the city even today.

182
00:14:05,121 --> 00:14:10,080
It was a city that was a work of art in itself,

183
00:14:10,300 --> 00:14:13,280
where the idea was to turn the bricks and mortar

184
00:14:13,281 --> 00:14:16,160
into a reflection of a certain set of principles and ideals.

185
00:14:16,940 --> 00:14:20,100
No project embodied those ideals more magnificently

186
00:14:20,101 --> 00:14:21,980
than Brunelleschi's dome,

187
00:14:22,580 --> 00:14:24,620
one of the era's great engineering marvels.

188
00:14:26,140 --> 00:14:28,780
Its structure stands more than 300 feet

189
00:14:28,781 --> 00:14:32,040
and is still the largest masonry-built dome in the world.

190
00:14:32,041 --> 00:14:40,720
In 1471, when Leonardo was 19 years old, the City Council of Florence commissioned the dome's final phase.

191
00:14:41,880 --> 00:14:51,120
To place a two-ton bronze orb on the dome's peak, the city elders turned to Andrea del Verrocchio, Leonardo's mentor and one of his greatest influences.

192
00:14:52,700 --> 00:14:56,640
To complete the task, Verrocchio employed a fleet of complex machines.

193
00:14:56,641 --> 00:15:04,800
standing at his side Leonardo marveled as giant hoists and cranes lifted the material skyward

194
00:15:04,801 --> 00:15:10,440
during the final stage the size and weight of the bronze orb required elaborate scaffolding

195
00:15:10,441 --> 00:15:17,460
and a crane that shifted the load and counterweight simultaneously to keep in perfect balance when the

196
00:15:17,461 --> 00:15:22,620
project was completed a year later Leonardo declared that mechanics truly was the paradise

197
00:15:22,621 --> 00:15:26,420
of all sciences.

198
00:15:26,421 --> 00:15:29,420
And in the years to come, Leonardo used the same pulleys,

199
00:15:29,421 --> 00:15:33,220
weights, and gears to create many inventions, including this

200
00:15:33,221 --> 00:15:39,920
early ventilation fan and this hydraulic power saw.

201
00:15:46,860 --> 00:15:49,780
Of particular interest were clocks.

202
00:15:49,781 --> 00:15:53,700
The way one action led to and followed another suggested many possibilities,

203
00:15:54,280 --> 00:15:57,660
not just for telling time, but for many other applications as well.

204
00:15:58,320 --> 00:16:01,840
And later, when conjoined with springs, foreshadowed Leonardo's robots.

205
00:16:03,580 --> 00:16:08,700
Engineer and author Mark Rosheim has spent a lifetime solving the mysteries of Leonardo's robots,

206
00:16:09,340 --> 00:16:14,180
beginning with this complex drawing that had baffled scholars until late in the 20th century.

207
00:16:15,220 --> 00:16:17,780
This is way before anything else.

208
00:16:17,781 --> 00:16:26,280
I mean, this is the earliest we know of a device like this, so it changes history, you know, changes the technological timeline.

209
00:16:27,120 --> 00:16:35,440
What the drawing reveals is a small cart that's self-propelled, programmable, and capable of moving in a series of different directions on its own.

210
00:16:36,860 --> 00:16:40,060
The clock-like mechanics reveal much about Leonardo's genius.

211
00:16:40,061 --> 00:16:45,420
Well, starting in the 1920s when this kind of got on the radar screen of historians,

212
00:16:45,920 --> 00:16:52,820
the thought was that these springs, these leaf springs, were what propelled the cart.

213
00:16:53,080 --> 00:16:58,640
And they would actually alter the drawing and have these things tied down to these corner cogs

214
00:16:58,641 --> 00:17:04,340
and they'd come up with these crazy reconstructions instead of looking at what Leonardo had actually done.

215
00:17:04,341 --> 00:17:10,800
Rossheim's mentor, scholar Carlo Pedretti, was the first to suggest that the large-toothed

216
00:17:10,801 --> 00:17:17,160
gearheads obscured something more, a pair of coil springs hidden beneath, the true source

217
00:17:17,161 --> 00:17:20,580
of the robot cart's power.

218
00:17:20,581 --> 00:17:25,040
The visible spring devices once assumed to be the power source were in fact the steering

219
00:17:25,041 --> 00:17:27,820
mechanism.

220
00:17:27,821 --> 00:17:34,820
My first discovery was that these pedals here were cams.

221
00:17:34,821 --> 00:17:41,920
They were faintly drawn and then I could see that these cams tripped this lever.

222
00:17:41,921 --> 00:17:48,320
And then I could deduce that this lever must be connected to the steering wheel.

223
00:17:48,321 --> 00:17:53,540
The larger the cams, the sharper the turn of the steering wheel, and vice versa.

224
00:17:53,541 --> 00:17:58,080
The cams could be placed in any order, allowing the engineer, in essence, to program the route

225
00:17:58,081 --> 00:18:01,560
the robot cart would take.

226
00:18:01,561 --> 00:18:04,040
Even speed could be regulated.

227
00:18:04,041 --> 00:18:09,180
After that, the question was, what did these things do, these cogs?

228
00:18:09,181 --> 00:18:13,260
And I could see this other spring here.

229
00:18:13,261 --> 00:18:16,540
These were behaving like a clock.

230
00:18:16,541 --> 00:18:18,700
This was the tick-tock of a clock.

231
00:18:18,701 --> 00:18:26,820
In other words, this was behaving like the pendulum of a clock and it was regulating the release of the energy in the spring.

232
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:29,640
In this case, the springs are underneath these big gears.

233
00:18:30,140 --> 00:18:37,500
The clock-like release of energy within the springs guaranteed the robot cart a smooth, steady ride as it moved through a room on its own.

234
00:18:38,440 --> 00:18:45,880
A remarkable sight in the 15th century, sure to wow Leonardo's patrons, just as we are astonished by today's robotic devices.

235
00:18:45,881 --> 00:18:55,320
The big thing is, it proves that the roots of technology go far deeper than we ever realized.

236
00:18:55,321 --> 00:19:05,000
That concepts of programmability, concepts of computerized robots go hundreds of years

237
00:19:05,001 --> 00:19:06,360
deeper than we ever thought.

238
00:19:07,960 --> 00:19:11,000
But Leonardo's cart was only the first of his robotic creations.

239
00:19:11,001 --> 00:19:19,140
While at the court of Milan, Leonardo was called on by the Duke to oversee the elaborate

240
00:19:19,141 --> 00:19:21,760
court pageants that played a vital role in his rule.

241
00:19:22,160 --> 00:19:24,740
The prince had to be surrounded by magnificence.

242
00:19:24,880 --> 00:19:30,820
He had to represent the wealth and culture and military prowess and dignity and even

243
00:19:30,821 --> 00:19:33,280
almost the anthropological bloodline of his people.

244
00:19:33,960 --> 00:19:37,040
And pageantry did this in an extraordinarily successful way.

245
00:19:37,380 --> 00:19:38,640
It wasn't just entertainment.

246
00:19:38,641 --> 00:19:40,160
It was propaganda.

247
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:46,000
In 1495, Leonardo unveiled a robot knight

248
00:19:46,100 --> 00:19:49,240
at one of the Duke celebrations.

249
00:19:49,340 --> 00:19:53,080
The robot knight could sit, stand, and raise its visor,

250
00:19:53,180 --> 00:19:56,640
all by means of an elaborate pulley and cabling system.

251
00:19:56,740 --> 00:20:01,620
There's evidence that it was capable of independent motion.

252
00:20:01,720 --> 00:20:02,860
It could go like this.

253
00:20:02,960 --> 00:20:07,020
There's also evidence that it may have had the ability

254
00:20:07,120 --> 00:20:09,760
to have a cable stretched across its two arms

255
00:20:09,761 --> 00:20:13,440
and that it could, for example, grab somebody.

256
00:20:14,800 --> 00:20:18,040
In 2002, Rosheim built the night robot,

257
00:20:18,660 --> 00:20:21,900
proving that Leonardo truly was a master of robotics.

258
00:20:22,540 --> 00:20:26,240
The machines are so, they're Swiss watch-like.

259
00:20:26,400 --> 00:20:29,880
I mean, there's so very few components doing many different roles,

260
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,200
and they're just so simple and so elegant.

261
00:20:32,920 --> 00:20:35,020
Nowhere would that elegance be more apparent

262
00:20:35,021 --> 00:20:38,900
than in Leonardo's attempts to conquer one of the greatest of man's challenges.

263
00:20:39,760 --> 00:20:42,360
Flight.

264
00:20:42,361 --> 00:20:45,120
To construct the night robot, Mark Rossheim and his team

265
00:20:45,121 --> 00:20:49,520
employed more than 20 pulleys, a modern car jack,

266
00:20:49,521 --> 00:20:52,460
and 46 feet of steel cable.

267
00:20:52,461 --> 00:20:54,880
DaVinci Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

268
00:21:01,560 --> 00:21:03,800
Flight.

269
00:21:03,801 --> 00:21:06,620
It's now so commonplace that many of us forget just what

270
00:21:06,621 --> 00:21:09,640
a threshold it once was.

271
00:21:09,641 --> 00:21:12,880
For Leonardo da Vinci, to fly was a dream that haunted him

272
00:21:12,881 --> 00:21:16,820
and inspired him to design a host of incredible flying machines.

273
00:21:18,320 --> 00:21:21,480
Today, many consider him the grandfather of modern aviation.

274
00:21:22,120 --> 00:21:25,780
So it was the first time that any kind of engineering discipline

275
00:21:25,781 --> 00:21:29,220
had been put into the design of an aircraft.

276
00:21:29,420 --> 00:21:33,040
Up to that point, it had been either mythology like Icarus and Daedalus

277
00:21:33,041 --> 00:21:37,220
or these poor guys that strap wings to themselves and jump off a tower or a wall.

278
00:21:37,720 --> 00:21:39,640
Leonardo da Vinci had thought about the materials.

279
00:21:40,340 --> 00:21:42,280
He thought about the sizing, the proportions.

280
00:21:43,200 --> 00:21:48,020
He observed nature and took from nature what had to be taken in his mind to make it work.

281
00:21:49,600 --> 00:21:55,060
Like the ancients before him, Leonardo's fascination with flight began with the observation of birds,

282
00:21:55,300 --> 00:21:56,860
which he studied for most of his life.

283
00:21:57,520 --> 00:22:01,380
In his mid-30s, Leonardo began making detailed drawings of birds,

284
00:22:01,840 --> 00:22:03,860
seeking to explain the dynamics of their wings.

285
00:22:03,861 --> 00:22:16,240
We know from his drawings and from his writings that he always started with concrete observations, direct observations, very precise.

286
00:22:16,500 --> 00:22:26,040
But it was not for the observation itself, but in order to penetrate something deeper, to go behind flight to find out what the essence was.

287
00:22:26,041 --> 00:22:32,600
Over time, Leonardo began to see patterns in the dynamics of a bird's wings that resembled

288
00:22:32,601 --> 00:22:36,280
aspects of his other interests, especially mechanics.

289
00:22:36,281 --> 00:22:42,160
A bird is an instrument working according to mathematical law, he wrote, and those laws

290
00:22:42,161 --> 00:22:47,620
were within the capacity of man to reproduce with all its movements.

291
00:22:47,621 --> 00:22:53,000
To prove his theory, Leonardo began to experiment, not with air, but with water.

292
00:22:53,001 --> 00:22:56,000
Leonardo was very fond of analogies.

293
00:22:56,001 --> 00:22:59,000
He saw a similarity between water and air.

294
00:22:59,001 --> 00:23:02,000
What he really wanted to know was how birds fly.

295
00:23:02,001 --> 00:23:05,000
However, doing experiments on birds is difficult.

296
00:23:05,001 --> 00:23:08,000
So Leonardo thought, let me do experiments on water.

297
00:23:08,001 --> 00:23:11,000
And one of the things he did was to put a stick in the water

298
00:23:11,001 --> 00:23:14,000
and examine how the water flows around it.

299
00:23:14,001 --> 00:23:18,000
If I hold it in this direction, it makes hardly any difference.

300
00:23:18,001 --> 00:23:20,000
However, if I hold it perpendicularly,

301
00:23:20,001 --> 00:23:22,000
you see the water climb up behind.

302
00:23:22,001 --> 00:23:27,440
And this was important for Leonardo because he knew if he tilted the stick at an angle,

303
00:23:27,540 --> 00:23:29,400
he could perhaps get some lift from it.

304
00:23:29,880 --> 00:23:33,920
So it doesn't take much for Leonardo with his powerful imagination to go from a straight stick

305
00:23:33,921 --> 00:23:39,680
to an Archimedean screw, to an Archimedean spiral, and hence to imagine a helicopter.

306
00:23:41,120 --> 00:23:46,660
The aerial screw, better known as Leonardo's helicopter, is one of the most famous of his drawings.

307
00:23:47,660 --> 00:23:49,560
It offers his usual detail.

308
00:23:49,561 --> 00:23:56,560
The screw had a diameter of more than 15 feet, and it was made of reed, linen, and wire.

309
00:23:56,561 --> 00:24:02,460
Four men would stand on the central platform and turn the cranks in front of them, causing

310
00:24:02,461 --> 00:24:06,440
the shaft to rotate and create lift.

311
00:24:06,441 --> 00:24:13,320
Its weight alone ensured that his design couldn't have worked, but it did get Leonardo thinking.

312
00:24:13,321 --> 00:24:18,680
Soon he began experimenting with a wing concept that more closely resembled those of birds,

313
00:24:18,681 --> 00:24:21,120
and later bats.

314
00:24:21,121 --> 00:24:23,640
These inquiries culminated in the designs

315
00:24:23,641 --> 00:24:27,060
for Leonardo's ornithopter, a flying machine that flapped

316
00:24:27,061 --> 00:24:29,160
its wings just like a bird.

317
00:24:29,161 --> 00:24:32,140
There are many things about bird flight and bat flight

318
00:24:32,141 --> 00:24:34,400
that seem on the face of it to be an inspiration

319
00:24:34,401 --> 00:24:35,880
for full-scale flight.

320
00:24:35,881 --> 00:24:39,180
They do it so effortlessly.

321
00:24:39,181 --> 00:24:41,660
Leonardo's ornithopter is a true original,

322
00:24:41,661 --> 00:24:44,860
the first in history.

323
00:24:44,861 --> 00:24:47,680
Based on several drawings, the composite design

324
00:24:47,681 --> 00:24:50,380
featured a two-winged airship powered by human strength.

325
00:24:51,240 --> 00:24:53,560
The wingspan exceeded 33 feet,

326
00:24:54,120 --> 00:24:56,380
slightly less than a classic modern Piper Cub.

327
00:24:58,400 --> 00:25:01,600
The skeleton frame of each wing was to be made of pine,

328
00:25:01,960 --> 00:25:04,300
then covered in raw silk and starched taffeta

329
00:25:04,301 --> 00:25:07,080
to create a light but sturdy membrane.

330
00:25:09,880 --> 00:25:13,000
For power, the pilot would pump a set of back pedals

331
00:25:13,001 --> 00:25:14,580
using an alternate leg motion.

332
00:25:15,460 --> 00:25:17,540
A hand crank increased the energy produced,

333
00:25:17,680 --> 00:25:20,840
while a head harness operated both the rudder and elevator,

334
00:25:21,500 --> 00:25:23,720
allowing the now very busy pilot to steer.

335
00:25:25,860 --> 00:25:29,660
Leonardo realized that a bird's feathers weren't necessary for flight.

336
00:25:30,260 --> 00:25:32,480
But in order to generate the needed lift and thrust,

337
00:25:32,880 --> 00:25:35,900
the ornithopter's wings would need to twist as they flapped.

338
00:25:37,180 --> 00:25:40,900
If you just have a flat wing, one that isn't capable of twisting,

339
00:25:41,400 --> 00:25:42,200
you'll go nowhere.

340
00:25:43,020 --> 00:25:44,000
It'll just stir up the air.

341
00:25:44,001 --> 00:25:48,380
For a wing design like da Vinci's, you have to have twist.

342
00:25:48,540 --> 00:25:49,680
I think he understood that.

343
00:25:49,940 --> 00:25:52,060
So that the wing would twist on the downstroke

344
00:25:52,061 --> 00:25:53,960
and twist the other way on the upstroke.

345
00:25:56,400 --> 00:25:59,240
Over time, Leonardo became so enthusiastic

346
00:25:59,241 --> 00:26:02,360
that he began to design additional elements to aid the pilot,

347
00:26:02,920 --> 00:26:05,320
including landing gear that featured shock absorbers.

348
00:26:06,660 --> 00:26:10,280
Such extras imply that Leonardo was serious about building the ornithopter.

349
00:26:10,281 --> 00:26:15,660
I think a lot of his devices and gadgets he didn't need to build.

350
00:26:16,100 --> 00:26:20,780
It was obvious to him from the drawing and from his wonderful imagination that they would work.

351
00:26:21,380 --> 00:26:26,300
However, he was very serious about building a flying machine, apparently built parts of it,

352
00:26:26,500 --> 00:26:31,260
but it must have struck him eventually that this thing just wouldn't work.

353
00:26:31,261 --> 00:26:43,940
Nearly 500 years later in 1991, Professor James Deloria and a team of his students launched

354
00:26:43,941 --> 00:26:51,280
the first remote-controlled engine-powered ornithopter in history.

355
00:26:51,281 --> 00:26:56,440
Inspired by Leonardo's designs, the scale model proved that a larger manned ornithopter

356
00:26:56,441 --> 00:26:59,500
was indeed possible.

357
00:26:59,501 --> 00:27:07,400
Today, Deloria is even closer to fulfilling Leonardo's dream.

358
00:27:07,401 --> 00:27:13,300
Housed at the Toronto Aerospace Museum, this full-scale manned ornithopter features a 42-foot

359
00:27:13,301 --> 00:27:20,180
wingspan, proprietary wing elements, and a powerful engine-driven central yoke that flaps

360
00:27:20,181 --> 00:27:26,900
the wings at a rate of 1.2 cycles per second, driving the ornithopter forward at more than

361
00:27:26,901 --> 00:27:29,180
50 miles per hour.

362
00:27:29,181 --> 00:27:35,080
But even if this design differs from Leonardo's original, the inspiration remains the same.

363
00:27:35,081 --> 00:27:38,360
What engineering is, it's a form of art.

364
00:27:38,361 --> 00:27:43,360
You're using the principles of science, the mathematics, but what you're getting is something

365
00:27:43,361 --> 00:27:44,360
brand new.

366
00:27:44,361 --> 00:27:46,840
That to me is the pleasure of engineering.

367
00:27:46,841 --> 00:27:53,020
And Leonardo da Vinci to me is the highest manifestation, highest example of that combination

368
00:27:53,021 --> 00:27:57,060
of creativity and engineering and art.

369
00:27:57,061 --> 00:28:00,660
After years of trying to create the ornithopter,

370
00:28:00,661 --> 00:28:04,020
Leonardo put aside the pursuit of flight,

371
00:28:04,021 --> 00:28:09,660
only to return to it later with fresh eyes.

372
00:28:09,661 --> 00:28:11,700
You look at the birds, they say,

373
00:28:11,701 --> 00:28:17,780
birds are not so big, they stay without any movement in the air, they float.

374
00:28:17,781 --> 00:28:23,100
Leonardo realized that the key to flight wasn't to build a machine that overcame the forces of nature,

375
00:28:23,101 --> 00:28:27,160
but to build one that utilized those forces, to float and to glide.

376
00:28:28,580 --> 00:28:34,200
It's exploiting the force not simply of man, but of the air itself, of air currents, of winds,

377
00:28:34,700 --> 00:28:39,780
and to obtain the force of man simply to correct that like Berdoux.

378
00:28:44,800 --> 00:28:52,280
In June 2000, daredevil Adrian Nicholas transformed a sketch Leonardo had made in the margin of one of his notebooks

379
00:28:52,281 --> 00:28:55,200
into a soaring tribute to da Vinci's genius.

380
00:28:57,480 --> 00:28:59,360
Nicholas designed Leonardo's parachute

381
00:28:59,361 --> 00:29:01,620
to the master's exact specifications.

382
00:29:02,540 --> 00:29:04,780
Because it weighed over 200 pounds,

383
00:29:05,160 --> 00:29:07,040
40 times more than a modern parachute,

384
00:29:07,720 --> 00:29:10,360
experts warned Nicholas that the design wouldn't work

385
00:29:10,361 --> 00:29:12,060
and that his life hung in the balance.

386
00:29:16,660 --> 00:29:19,100
But Leonardo's parachute did work.

387
00:29:19,101 --> 00:29:24,360
and Nicholas soared for more than five minutes,

388
00:29:25,140 --> 00:29:27,600
a grand gesture that proved again

389
00:29:27,601 --> 00:29:30,600
that Leonardo da Vinci should never be underestimated.

390
00:29:35,420 --> 00:29:38,260
Leonardo's time in Milan would soon come to an end,

391
00:29:38,520 --> 00:29:40,200
but not before he undertook

392
00:29:40,201 --> 00:29:43,100
the greatest engineering challenge of his career.

393
00:29:45,360 --> 00:29:48,300
In 1901, a 12-year-old Russian boy

394
00:29:48,301 --> 00:29:50,240
saw Leonardo's mechanical drawings,

395
00:29:50,241 --> 00:29:52,540
and they changed his life.

396
00:29:52,541 --> 00:29:55,100
Igor Sikorsky later built one of the first working

397
00:29:55,101 --> 00:29:56,300
helicopters in history.

398
00:30:02,700 --> 00:30:05,300
From the time cities first arose,

399
00:30:05,301 --> 00:30:09,160
leaders have filled them with monuments and statues,

400
00:30:09,161 --> 00:30:12,040
public symbols that can unite not just individuals,

401
00:30:12,041 --> 00:30:16,360
but nations, and even generations.

402
00:30:16,361 --> 00:30:21,560
In 1966, an American researcher working in Madrid made a startling discovery.

403
00:30:22,860 --> 00:30:26,300
Two lost Leonardo notebooks, not seen for generations.

404
00:30:28,780 --> 00:30:31,920
Inside were plans for a Renaissance-era colossus,

405
00:30:32,440 --> 00:30:36,020
a statue that would stand more than 24 feet tall,

406
00:30:36,021 --> 00:30:38,440
four times the height of an average man,

407
00:30:38,940 --> 00:30:42,040
and would require more than 80 tons of bronze to cast.

408
00:30:43,320 --> 00:30:45,840
It was known as Leonardo's horse,

409
00:30:46,360 --> 00:30:49,460
the most difficult artistic and engineering challenge of his career.

410
00:30:50,220 --> 00:30:53,300
This is complexity 360 degrees.

411
00:30:53,540 --> 00:30:56,440
You have anatomy, you have proportion, you have technical problem,

412
00:30:56,560 --> 00:30:59,860
you have infrastructural problem, you have economical problem

413
00:30:59,861 --> 00:31:01,960
because the amount of bronze was so much.

414
00:31:04,840 --> 00:31:07,020
Beginning in the early 1480s,

415
00:31:07,620 --> 00:31:10,120
Leonardo worked on the project for more than a decade,

416
00:31:10,500 --> 00:31:13,740
about three times longer than it took to paint The Last Supper.

417
00:31:13,741 --> 00:31:14,441
him.

418
00:31:14,442 --> 00:31:19,522
But for all his care and planning, Leonardo didn't live to see the horse completed.

419
00:31:19,523 --> 00:31:20,223
It became

420
00:31:20,224 --> 00:31:26,540
another of his lost dreams, until it, too, was reawakened nearly 500 years later by an American

421
00:31:26,541 --> 00:31:28,611
named Charles C. Dent.

422
00:31:28,612 --> 00:31:35,200
My uncle read this story about how Leonardo on his deathbed reportedly

423
00:31:35,201 --> 00:31:38,749
wept about not being able to finish the horse.

424
00:31:38,750 --> 00:31:42,220
He decided, let's give Leonardo his horse and

425
00:31:42,221 --> 00:31:44,180
do this as a

426
00:31:44,181 --> 00:31:46,740
tribute of thanks to the Italian people,

427
00:31:47,120 --> 00:31:48,620
sort of like a statue

428
00:31:48,621 --> 00:31:50,600
of liberty going in a

429
00:31:50,601 --> 00:31:52,300
reverse direction. Dent,

430
00:31:52,560 --> 00:31:54,520
a retired airline pilot and passionate

431
00:31:54,521 --> 00:31:56,400
art collector, decided to build

432
00:31:56,401 --> 00:31:58,500
Leonardo's horse by staying as true to the

433
00:31:58,501 --> 00:31:59,780
master's vision as possible.

434
00:32:03,480 --> 00:32:04,440
In time,

435
00:32:04,540 --> 00:32:05,760
others would join that quest,

436
00:32:06,340 --> 00:32:08,260
including sculptor Nina Okamu.

437
00:32:08,261 --> 00:32:16,100
The creation of a statue of this magnitude begins in clay, in order to finalize the design.

438
00:32:18,500 --> 00:32:21,580
Leonardo's notebooks are filled with drawings of how horses move.

439
00:32:22,440 --> 00:32:26,600
At first, Leonardo wanted his statue to rear up, a revolutionary idea.

440
00:32:27,180 --> 00:32:30,500
But the engineering challenges were simply too great to overcome.

441
00:32:31,700 --> 00:32:34,220
The stresses on the legs would have been just too much.

442
00:32:34,221 --> 00:32:37,500
It would not have been able to be supported on its own,

443
00:32:37,501 --> 00:32:43,940
or also it could tip and fall over a plethora of difficulties

444
00:32:43,941 --> 00:32:47,400
with 80 tons on just two supporting points.

445
00:32:48,420 --> 00:32:51,500
Eventually, Leonardo settled on a more traditional pose.

446
00:32:53,100 --> 00:32:57,100
Once finalized, Dent's team needed a full-scale clay model

447
00:32:57,101 --> 00:32:58,680
to begin the casting process.

448
00:32:59,740 --> 00:33:02,840
Great care was taken to ensure that this contemporary version

449
00:33:02,841 --> 00:33:06,240
mirrored Leonardo's own 24-foot-high clay model,

450
00:33:06,780 --> 00:33:09,480
which he unveiled to the Duke of Milan in 1493.

451
00:33:12,380 --> 00:33:15,320
Next, Leonardo would have had to solve the many challenges

452
00:33:15,321 --> 00:33:18,520
posed by controlling the 80 tons of molten bronze

453
00:33:18,521 --> 00:33:20,200
needed to cast the horse.

454
00:33:21,120 --> 00:33:23,300
One of the things that he was very concerned with

455
00:33:23,301 --> 00:33:25,240
was this huge pour of bronze

456
00:33:25,241 --> 00:33:29,680
was to be able to control the thickness of the bronze.

457
00:33:30,160 --> 00:33:31,740
The thickness that they wanted to have

458
00:33:31,741 --> 00:33:33,460
was basically two fingers thick.

459
00:33:35,680 --> 00:33:37,220
Without the proper thickness,

460
00:33:37,400 --> 00:33:39,460
the statue would suffer from a fatal instability.

461
00:33:40,160 --> 00:33:43,160
So Leonardo devised an ingenious mold-making technique,

462
00:33:43,580 --> 00:33:45,840
drawing on the lessons of his earlier weapons designs.

463
00:33:46,960 --> 00:33:52,180
He adopted a number of techniques for making cannon,

464
00:33:52,720 --> 00:33:56,760
utilizing a technology for a variety of molds

465
00:33:56,761 --> 00:34:00,620
that were really only used in military to bring it into art.

466
00:34:00,621 --> 00:34:06,660
And this was really quite fundamentally different from things that had been done up to that point in time.

467
00:34:07,240 --> 00:34:13,740
The process begins with the creation of a master mold by coating the clay model in a hard, fast-drying material.

468
00:34:14,780 --> 00:34:19,480
Here technicians used rubber, while in Leonardo's time a unique plaster formula was used.

469
00:34:24,040 --> 00:34:29,420
Once the coating dries, the clay model is removed, leaving a hard plaster master mold.

470
00:34:30,620 --> 00:34:36,320
The inside of the mold is then covered with a uniform layer of wax, creating what Leonardo called thickness.

471
00:34:38,480 --> 00:34:42,260
Finally, a third layer, this time a fire clay, was applied.

472
00:34:44,120 --> 00:34:48,680
The master mold is now ready to be heated to more than 2,200 degrees Fahrenheit,

473
00:34:49,180 --> 00:34:52,440
which causes the wax to melt and seep out the bottom of the mold.

474
00:34:53,800 --> 00:34:59,200
Molten bronze is then poured inside the existing gap, where it will harden and form the walls of the statue.

475
00:35:00,620 --> 00:35:09,720
Today, a sculpture the size of the horse is divided into sections, which are cast individually

476
00:35:09,721 --> 00:35:16,820
and welded together using modern joining techniques.

477
00:35:16,821 --> 00:35:26,060
For Leonardo, however, the challenge was to create the statue all at once in a single

478
00:35:26,061 --> 00:35:27,541
pour of bronze.

479
00:35:27,542 --> 00:35:35,440
Because of the size of the horse, no single furnace could deliver enough heat to

480
00:35:35,441 --> 00:35:40,288
ensure the liquid bronze would properly seep into every crevice.

481
00:35:40,289 --> 00:35:42,560
So Leonardo designed a complex

482
00:35:42,561 --> 00:35:47,100
series of pipes and ovens that would need to burn for more than a week to reach the requisite

483
00:35:47,101 --> 00:35:55,920
temperatures. Eventually, Leonardo resolved nearly every technical challenge for the

484
00:35:55,921 --> 00:36:01,360
completion of the horse and was preparing for its casting when history took a dark turn.

485
00:36:02,720 --> 00:36:06,452
In 1494, King Charles VIII of France invaded Italy.

486
00:36:06,453 --> 00:36:09,160
To forestall the capture of his city,

487
00:36:09,860 --> 00:36:16,460
Duke Sforza bribed the French with all 80 tons of Leonardo's bronze, which the French then turned

488
00:36:16,461 --> 00:36:21,124
into cannon. In 1499, the French returned.

489
00:36:21,125 --> 00:36:25,900
This time, the Duke fled, and Leonardo's 28

490
00:36:25,901 --> 00:36:29,400
foot high clay model was mocked by French soldiers.

491
00:36:30,880 --> 00:36:37,900
The clay model is reported by Echronica to be destroyed by the bows of the French soldiers

492
00:36:37,901 --> 00:36:43,240
arriving in Milan, taking this as a funny target against the Sporza regime after its

493
00:36:43,241 --> 00:36:43,941
collapse.

494
00:36:44,460 --> 00:36:49,820
In his notebooks, Leonardo records only a single painful sentence, I will speak of the

495
00:36:49,821 --> 00:36:50,780
horse no more.

496
00:36:50,781 --> 00:36:57,140
almost 500 years later in 1992

497
00:36:57,141 --> 00:37:01,120
Charles Dent was receiving international attention for his work

498
00:37:01,121 --> 00:37:03,620
and the horse's completion appeared within reach

499
00:37:03,621 --> 00:37:09,620
but in 1994 Dent was diagnosed with Lou Gehrig's disease

500
00:37:09,621 --> 00:37:11,320
and the end came quickly

501
00:37:11,321 --> 00:37:17,020
like Leonardo Charlie never saw the horse completed in his lifetime

502
00:37:17,021 --> 00:37:26,020
and a group of us promised him before he died that we would finish the horse for him.

503
00:37:29,020 --> 00:37:36,020
Five years later, on September 10, 1999, Leonardo's horse was finally unveiled in Milan,

504
00:37:36,021 --> 00:37:40,020
a moment that celebrated not only Leonardo and Charles Dent,

505
00:37:40,021 --> 00:37:43,020
but a creative legacy that binds two nations.

506
00:37:47,020 --> 00:37:55,820
But beyond the great statue, Leonardo's vision would lead us beneath the sea, and even into

507
00:37:55,821 --> 00:38:00,820
space.

508
00:38:00,821 --> 00:38:05,500
Had it been completed, Leonardo's 80-ton bronze horse would have weighed as much as 50 Ferrari

509
00:38:05,501 --> 00:38:10,940
sports cars, or 13 of Michelangelo's Davids.

510
00:38:10,941 --> 00:38:13,420
Da Vinci Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

511
00:38:17,020 --> 00:38:27,220
In 1994, the last of Leonardo's notebooks, still held in private hands, was put up for sale.

512
00:38:27,221 --> 00:38:38,800
At $10 million, $11 million, $12 million, in the room at $12 million, $13 million on the telephone, $14 million.

513
00:38:38,801 --> 00:38:43,200
The bidding at Christie's auction house began at $5.6 million,

514
00:38:43,820 --> 00:38:47,140
but in less than four minutes, it rose to $28 million.

515
00:38:47,720 --> 00:38:50,200
On the telephone in this room, $28 million.

516
00:38:50,720 --> 00:38:53,660
Last call, $28 million. Thank you.

517
00:38:55,360 --> 00:38:59,620
The buyer, Bill Gates, one of the world's richest men.

518
00:39:01,160 --> 00:39:03,300
Now known as the Codex Leicester,

519
00:39:03,960 --> 00:39:07,400
the notebook attests to Leonardo's deep fascination with water,

520
00:39:07,940 --> 00:39:10,540
The essence of life, its rivers,

521
00:39:11,200 --> 00:39:13,440
indispensable thoroughfares in Renaissance Italy.

522
00:39:14,620 --> 00:39:17,800
We have to remember that in the Renaissance,

523
00:39:18,460 --> 00:39:21,980
waterways were the main means of communication.

524
00:39:22,760 --> 00:39:24,660
Canals, rivers.

525
00:39:25,580 --> 00:39:27,960
This was the way in which you communicated.

526
00:39:31,340 --> 00:39:34,400
While in Milan, Leonardo spent many days

527
00:39:34,401 --> 00:39:36,040
at the Duke's estate in Vigivano,

528
00:39:36,041 --> 00:39:38,700
a small town just outside the main city.

529
00:39:42,120 --> 00:39:44,420
Here, amid many rivers and streams,

530
00:39:44,640 --> 00:39:46,380
Leonardo pursued his study of water,

531
00:39:47,180 --> 00:39:49,740
how it flowed, whirled, and eddied.

532
00:39:51,180 --> 00:39:53,420
These movements would reappear in his art,

533
00:39:53,800 --> 00:39:55,600
and they became the driving forces

534
00:39:55,601 --> 00:39:57,260
behind many of his larger inventions.

535
00:40:01,200 --> 00:40:04,340
In this design, water powered a large machine

536
00:40:04,341 --> 00:40:07,600
to grind and polish the surface of two mirrors simultaneously,

537
00:40:08,600 --> 00:40:11,160
speeding up a process traditionally done by hand.

538
00:40:14,240 --> 00:40:18,540
In 1499, with his horse in ruin and the Duke of Milan in exile,

539
00:40:19,340 --> 00:40:21,760
Leonardo left the city in search of new patronage.

540
00:40:22,940 --> 00:40:25,100
In 1500, he arrived in Venice

541
00:40:25,101 --> 00:40:27,420
and quickly assumed a familiar role,

542
00:40:27,940 --> 00:40:29,420
that of a military engineer.

543
00:40:30,800 --> 00:40:34,020
For the water city, Leonardo created a secret weapon.

544
00:40:34,340 --> 00:40:37,460
a leather diving suit for sneak attacks on enemy ships.

545
00:40:38,540 --> 00:40:40,620
Although not the first of its kind,

546
00:40:40,920 --> 00:40:43,100
it included the typical Leonardo details.

547
00:40:45,280 --> 00:40:47,520
Air was drawn through cane tubes

548
00:40:47,521 --> 00:40:50,200
attached to a small floating cork diving belt.

549
00:40:51,140 --> 00:40:53,160
A sealed leather bag with a valve

550
00:40:53,161 --> 00:40:56,140
could be inflated or deflated to surface or dive.

551
00:40:56,141 --> 00:41:08,800
But of all Leonardo's water projects, none was grander than his plan to redirect the Arno River,

552
00:41:09,720 --> 00:41:14,500
which ran through his native Florence to the port of Pisa and then into the Mediterranean.

553
00:41:15,980 --> 00:41:20,680
Leonardo had returned to Florence in 1501, where he befriended Niccolo Machiavelli,

554
00:41:21,120 --> 00:41:24,960
one of history's great political insiders and a man who dreamed big.

555
00:41:24,961 --> 00:41:35,360
Leonardo and Machiavelli had this great plan to build a waterway that would reach the Tyranian Sea

556
00:41:35,361 --> 00:41:40,700
and that would bypass the city of Pisa, which was one of their great competitors.

557
00:41:42,480 --> 00:41:48,400
Without the Arno River, Pisa's commercial and strategic importance as a port would simply evaporate

558
00:41:48,401 --> 00:41:52,240
and the city would become vulnerable to takeover by the Florentines.

559
00:41:54,960 --> 00:41:57,680
The initial plan was to channel the river southward,

560
00:41:58,400 --> 00:42:02,700
using weirs, dams, and a mile-long, 32-foot-deep ditch.

561
00:42:03,840 --> 00:42:06,240
By Leonardo's own estimates, construction of the ditch

562
00:42:06,241 --> 00:42:09,260
would require more than a million tons of earth to be moved,

563
00:42:10,040 --> 00:42:12,840
taking more than 54,000 man days.

564
00:42:13,660 --> 00:42:16,460
Heavy machines of his own design would supplement the workers.

565
00:42:17,480 --> 00:42:19,520
Construction actually started on the project,

566
00:42:19,840 --> 00:42:22,080
but heavy rain soon washed away the work.

567
00:42:22,081 --> 00:42:28,200
A year later, Leonardo tried to revive the project with a new set of plans, more elaborate

568
00:42:28,201 --> 00:42:33,620
than the first, including a much grander canal with mills.

569
00:42:33,621 --> 00:42:38,380
It was to be, in essence, a new industrial center under Florentine control.

570
00:42:38,381 --> 00:42:42,520
And he was making calculations in his, this would cost lots of money, he was saying to

571
00:42:42,521 --> 00:42:43,520
his patrons.

572
00:42:43,521 --> 00:42:50,020
But look, if you put there mills, if you make transportation by surface, and in five years

573
00:42:50,021 --> 00:42:51,900
you will recover.

574
00:42:51,901 --> 00:42:56,200
But again, Leonardo's grand project was abandoned.

575
00:42:56,201 --> 00:42:59,100
From then on, Leonardo's attention turned inward,

576
00:42:59,101 --> 00:43:04,120
toward his art and to the search for the essence

577
00:43:04,121 --> 00:43:09,780
of human beings, literally.

578
00:43:09,781 --> 00:43:11,720
Here in this recently discovered room

579
00:43:11,721 --> 00:43:15,860
hidden in a Florence monastery, Leonardo put down his brush

580
00:43:15,861 --> 00:43:20,660
and picked up the scalpel, conducting more than 30 autopsies.

581
00:43:20,661 --> 00:43:26,460
Refrigeration was not available, and tools were at times crude, resulting in an often gruesome process.

582
00:43:28,160 --> 00:43:34,860
But the detail of Leonardo's work reveals the depth of his curiosity, and that commitment continues to inspire.

583
00:43:35,980 --> 00:43:45,700
He's the father of kinesiology, the way human joints move, the father of programmable computers, on and on and on and on.

584
00:43:45,701 --> 00:43:51,640
And when I went to design my humanoid robot for NASA,

585
00:43:51,641 --> 00:43:53,900
I turned to his anatomical drawings

586
00:43:53,901 --> 00:43:56,680
because he drew them from the standpoint of an engineer.

587
00:43:57,120 --> 00:44:00,720
Today, we're experiencing a renaissance of interest in the master,

588
00:44:01,200 --> 00:44:05,060
and we're better able than ever to understand and assess his dreams and inventions.

589
00:44:05,780 --> 00:44:09,020
Yet our focus isn't simply on what was possible in Leonardo's time.

590
00:44:09,560 --> 00:44:11,940
It's also concentrated on the future.

591
00:44:11,941 --> 00:44:17,940
One day, Mark Rosheim's robotic devices might be a part of a NASA mission to Mars.

592
00:44:17,941 --> 00:44:22,940
Imagine that, the secret worlds of Leonardo in space.

593
00:44:41,940 --> 00:44:43,420
Deedeed

594
00:44:43,421 --> 00:44:44,900
